.Sh DESCRIPTION
The symmetric cipher commands allow data to be encrypted or decrypted
using various block and stream ciphers using keys based on passwords or
explicitly provided.
Base64 encoding or decoding can also be performed either by itself or in
addition to the encryption or decryption.
.Sh OPTIONS
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Sy - Ns Em cipher
The cipher to use.
.It Sy -help
Print out a usage message.
.It Sy -list
List all supported ciphers.
.It Sy -ciphers
Alias of -list to display all supported ciphers.
.It Sy -in Em filename
The input filename, standard input by default.
.It Sy -out Em filename
The output filename, standard output by default.
.It Sy -pass Em arg
The password source.
For more information about the format of
.Em arg
see
.Xr openssl-passphrase-options 1 .
.It Sy -e
Encrypt the input data: this is the default.
.It Sy -d
Decrypt the input data.
.It Sy -a
Base64 process the data.
This means that if encryption is taking place the data is base64 encoded
after encryption.
If decryption is set then the input data is base64 decoded before being
decrypted.
.Pp
When the
.Sy -A
option not given, on encoding a newline is inserted after each 64
characters, and on decoding a newline is expected among the first 1024
bytes of input.
.It Sy -base64
Same as
.Sy -a
.It Sy -A
If the
.Sy -a
option is set then base64 encoding produces output without any newline
character, and base64 decoding does not require any newlines.
Therefore it can be helpful to use the
.Sy -A
option when decoding unknown input.
.It Sy -k Em password
The password to derive the key from.
This is for compatibility with previous versions of OpenSSL. Superseded
by the
.Sy -pass
argument.
.It Sy -kfile Em filename
Read the password to derive the key from the first line of
.Em filename .
This is for compatibility with previous versions of OpenSSL. Superseded
by the
.Sy -pass
argument.
.It Sy -md Em digest
Use the specified digest to create the key from the passphrase.
The default algorithm is sha-256.
.It Sy -iter Em count
Use a given number of iterations on the password in deriving the
encryption key.
High values increase the time required to brute-force the resulting
file.
This option enables the use of PBKDF2 algorithm to derive the key.
.It Sy -pbkdf2
Use PBKDF2 algorithm with a default iteration count of 10000 unless
otherwise specified by the
.Sy -iter
command line option.
.It Sy -saltlen
Set the salt length to use when using the
.Sy -pbkdf2
option.
For compatibility reasons, the default is 8 bytes.
The maximum value is currently 16 bytes.
If the
.Sy -pbkdf2
option is not used, then this option is ignored and a fixed salt length
of 8 is used.
The salt length used when encrypting must also be used when decrypting.
.It Sy -nosalt
Don't use a salt in the key derivation routines.
This option
.Sy SHOULD NOT
be used except for test purposes or compatibility with ancient versions
of OpenSSL.
.It Sy -salt
Use salt (randomly generated or provide with
.Sy -S
option) when encrypting, this is the default.
.It Sy -S Em salt
The actual salt to use: this must be represented as a string of hex
digits.
If this option is used while encrypting, the same exact value will be
needed again during decryption.
This salt may be truncated or zero padded to match the salt length (See
.Sy -saltlen Ns ).
.It Sy -K Em key
The actual key to use: this must be represented as a string comprised
only of hex digits.
If only the key is specified, the IV must additionally specified using
the
.Sy -iv
option.
When both a key and a password are specified, the key given with the
.Sy -K
option will be used and the IV generated from the password will be
taken.
It does not make much sense to specify both key and password.
.It Sy -iv Em IV
The actual IV to use: this must be represented as a string comprised
only of hex digits.
When only the key is specified using the
.Sy -K
option, the IV must explicitly be defined.
When a password is being specified using one of the other options, the
IV is generated from this password.
.It Sy -p
Print out the key and IV used.
.It Sy -P
Print out the key and IV used then immediately exit: don't do any
encryption or decryption.
.It Sy -bufsize Em number Ns [ Ns Sy k ]
Set the buffer size for I/O. The maximum size that can be specified is
.Sy 2^31-1
(2147483647) bytes.
The
.Sy k
suffix can be specified to indicate that
.Em number
is provided in kibibytes (multiples of 1024 bytes).
.It Sy -nopad
Disable standard block padding.
.It Sy -v
Verbose print; display some statistics about I/O and buffer sizes.
.It Sy -debug
Debug the BIOs used for I/O.
.It Sy -z
Compress or decompress encrypted data using zlib after encryption or
before decryption.
This option exists only if OpenSSL was compiled with the zlib or
zlib-dynamic option.
.It Sy -none
Use NULL cipher (no encryption or decryption of input).
.It Sy -skeymgmt Em skeymgmt
Some providers may support opaque symmetric keys objects.
To use them, we need to know the name of the
.Sy EVP_SKEYMGMT
to be used.
If not specified, the name of the cipher will be used.
.Pp
To find out the name of the suitable symmetric key management, please
refer to the output of the
.Qo Li openssl list -skey-managers Qc
command.
.It Sy -skeyopt Em opt Ns : Ns Em value
To obtain an existing opaque symmetric key or generate a new one, key
options are specified as opt:value.
These options can't be used together with any options implying raw key
directly or indirectly.
.It Sy -rand Em files Ns , Sy -writerand Em file
See
.Xr openssl 1
for details.
.It Sy -provider Em name
.It Sy -provider-path Em path
.It Sy -provparam Em [name:]key=value
.It Sy -propquery Em propq
See
.Xr openssl 1 ,
.Xr provider 7 ,
and
.Xr property 7 .
.It Sy -engine Em id
See
.Xr openssl 1 .
This option is deprecated.
.El
.Sh NOTES
The program can be called either as
.Qo Li openssl Em cipher Qc
or
.Qo Li openssl enc - Ns Em cipher Qc .
The first form doesn't work with engine-provided ciphers, because this
form is processed before the configuration file is read and any ENGINEs
loaded.
Use the
.Xr openssl-list 1
command to get a list of supported ciphers.
.Pp
Engines which provide entirely new encryption algorithms (such as the
ccgost engine which provides gost89 algorithm) should be configured in
the configuration file.
Engines specified on the command line using
.Sy -engine
option can only be used for hardware-assisted implementations of ciphers
which are supported by the OpenSSL core or another engine specified in
the configuration file.
.Pp
When the enc command lists supported ciphers, ciphers provided by
engines, specified in the configuration files are listed too.
.Pp
A password will be prompted for to derive the key and IV if necessary.
.Pp
The
.Sy -salt
option should
.Sy ALWAYS
be used if the key is being derived from a password unless you want
compatibility with previous versions of OpenSSL.
.Pp
Without the
.Sy -salt
option it is possible to perform efficient dictionary attacks on the
password and to attack stream cipher encrypted data.
The reason for this is that without the salt the same password always
generates the same encryption key.
.Pp
When the salt is generated at random (that means when encrypting using a
passphrase without explicit salt given using
.Sy -S
option), the first bytes of the encrypted data are reserved to store the
salt for later decrypting.
.Pp
Some of the ciphers do not have large keys and others have security
implications if not used correctly.
A beginner is advised to just use a strong block cipher, such as AES, in
CBC mode.
.Pp
All the block ciphers normally use PKCS#7 padding, also known as
standard block padding.
This allows a rudimentary integrity or password check to be performed.
However, since the chance of random data passing the test is better than
1 in 256 it isn't a very good test.
.Pp
If padding is disabled then the input data must be a multiple of the
cipher block length.
.Pp
All RC2 ciphers have the same key and effective key length.
.Pp
Blowfish and RC5 algorithms use a 128 bit key.
.Pp
Please note that OpenSSL 3.0 changed the effect of the
.Sy -S
option.
Any explicit salt value specified via this option is no longer prepended
to the ciphertext when encrypting, and must again be explicitly provided
when decrypting.
Conversely, when the
.Sy -S
option is used during decryption, the ciphertext is expected to not have
a prepended salt value.
.Pp
When using OpenSSL 3.0 or later to decrypt data that was encrypted with
an explicit salt under OpenSSL 1.1.1 do not use the
.Sy -S
option, the salt will then be read from the ciphertext.
To generate ciphertext that can be decrypted with OpenSSL 1.1.1 do not
use the
.Sy -S
option, the salt will be then be generated randomly and prepended to the
output.
.Sh SUPPORTED CIPHERS
Note that some of these ciphers can be disabled at compile time and some
are available only if an appropriate engine is configured in the
configuration file.
The output when invoking this command with the
.Sy -list
option (that is
.Qo Li openssl enc -list Qc )
is a list of ciphers, supported by your version of OpenSSL, including
ones provided by configured engines.
.Pp
This command does not support authenticated encryption modes like CCM
and GCM, and will not support such modes in the future.
This is due to having to begin streaming output (e.g., to standard
output when
.Sy -out
is not used) before the authentication tag could be validated.
When this command is used in a pipeline, the receiving end will not be
able to roll back upon authentication failure.
The AEAD modes currently in common use also suffer from catastrophic
failure of confidentiality and/or integrity upon reuse of key/iv/nonce,
and since
.Sy openssl enc
places the entire burden of key/iv/nonce management upon the user, the
risk of exposing AEAD modes is too great to allow.
These key/iv/nonce management issues also affect other modes currently
exposed in this command, but the failure modes are less extreme in these
cases, and the functionality cannot be removed with a stable release
branch.
For bulk encryption of data, whether using authenticated encryption
modes or other modes,
.Xr openssl-cms 1
is recommended, as it provides a standard data format and performs the
needed key/iv/nonce management.
.Pp
When enc is used with key wrapping modes the input data cannot be
streamed, meaning it must be processed in a single pass.
Consequently, the input data size must be less than the buffer size
(-bufsize arg, default to 8*1024 bytes).
The '*-wrap' ciphers require the input to be a multiple of 8 bytes long,
because no padding is involved.
The '*-wrap-pad' ciphers allow any input length.
In both cases, no IV is needed.
See example below.
