.Sh EXAMPLES
Just base64 encode a binary file:
.Bd -literal
 openssl base64 -in file.bin -out file.b64
.Ed
.Pp
Decode the same file
.Bd -literal
 openssl base64 -d -in file.b64 -out file.bin
.Ed
.Pp
Encrypt a file using AES-128 using a prompted password and PBKDF2 key
derivation:
.Bd -literal
 openssl enc -aes128 -pbkdf2 -in file.txt -out file.aes128
.Ed
.Pp
Decrypt a file using a supplied password:
.Bd -literal
 openssl enc -aes128 -pbkdf2 -d -in file.aes128 -out file.txt \e
    -pass pass:<password>
.Ed
.Pp
Encrypt a file then base64 encode it (so it can be sent via mail for
example) using AES-256 in CTR mode and PBKDF2 key derivation:
.Bd -literal
 openssl enc -aes-256-ctr -pbkdf2 -a -in file.txt -out file.aes256
.Ed
.Pp
Base64 decode a file then decrypt it using a password supplied in a
file:
.Bd -literal
 openssl enc -aes-256-ctr -pbkdf2 -d -a -in file.aes256 -out file.txt \e
    -pass file:<passfile>
.Ed
.Pp
AES key wrapping:
.Bd -literal
 openssl enc -e -a -id-aes128-wrap-pad -K 000102030405060708090A0B0C0D0E0F -in file.bin
or
 openssl aes128-wrap-pad -e -a -K 000102030405060708090A0B0C0D0E0F -in file.bin
.Ed
.Sh BUGS
The
.Sy -A
option when used with large files doesn't work properly.
On the other hand, when base64 decoding without the
.Sy -A
option, if the first 1024 bytes of input do not include a newline
character the first two lines of input are ignored.
.Pp
The
.Sy openssl enc
command only supports a fixed number of algorithms with certain
parameters.
So if, for example, you want to use RC2 with a 76 bit key or RC4 with an
84 bit key you can't use this program.
